          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/" rel="author noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Author and lab tester</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">May 28, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Best For: Serious Hill Training</span>
          {PRODUCT.editorialScore && (<p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1 mb-0">Our editorial score: <strong class="text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.editorialScore}</strong>/5</p>)}
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The Commercial 2450 is the treadmill we'd point serious runners
          toward once they've outgrown the 1750. The stronger motor and 15% incline ceiling are real, meaningful upgrades for hard training —
          the question is whether your training actually needs them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
            <a href="#overview" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Overview and Who It's For</a>
            <a href="#specs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Full Spec Sheet</a>
            <a href="#assembly" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Assembly and Setup</a>
            <a href="#performance" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Performance Deep Dive</a>
            <a href="#durability" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a>
            <a href="#pros-cons" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Pros and Cons</a>
            <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. How It Compares</a>
            <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a>
            <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Final Verdict</a>
            <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every NordicTrack shopper eventually runs into the same question:
          is the Commercial 1750 enough, or do you need to spend more? The
          Commercial 2450 is NordicTrack's answer for the specific runner who
          outgrows the 1750, and comparing the two spec sheets side by side gives a clear picture of exactly who that upgrade makes sense for — and who it doesn't.
        </p>

        <div id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The Commercial 2450 occupies the middle of NordicTrack's
          Commercial line, sitting above the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/">Commercial 1750</a>
          and below the flagship
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2950-treadmill/">Commercial 2950</a>.
          It's built specifically for the buyer who has already decided the
          1750's fundamentals are right — the deck, the frame, the general
          shape of the machine — but wants a stronger motor and a steeper
          incline ceiling for genuinely demanding training.
        </p>
        <p>
          We structured this review as a direct comparison against the
          1750, since that's the real decision most shoppers reading this
          page are facing. Our conclusion from the specs: the difference matters specifically for hard incline work and sustained fast running, where the extra motor reserve and the higher incline ceiling come into play. During moderate walking or easy jogging, both motors are well within their range, so the extra $500 is genuinely hard to justify on performance grounds alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is still a folding treadmill using the same SpaceSaver design
          as the rest of the Commercial line, with a slightly larger folded
          footprint than the 1750 due to the bigger 24-inch touchscreen
          mast. It's built for a dedicated home gym space rather than an
          apartment that needs the treadmill to disappear daily, the same
          positioning as its siblings.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the Commercial 2450 most strongly to a runner
          training for a race that involves elevation, someone doing
          structured hill-repeat workouts regularly, or anyone who wants more motor reserve for push-pace intervals than a 3.75 CHP machine offers. If none
          of that describes your training, read our
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/">Commercial 1750 review</a>
          of the step-down model before committing to the extra spend here.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's worth being specific about what "outgrowing the 1750" looks
          like in practice, since that phrase gets thrown around loosely.
          We haven't tested either machine, but the logic is straightforward: back-to-back incline intervals at the 1750's 12% ceiling ask its 3.75 CHP motor to work near the top of its intended range rather than in the middle of it. That's not a defect on the 1750; it's simply what a ceiling means. The 2450 exists precisely to move that
          workload back into the middle of the motor's comfortable range,
          which matters if you're doing that kind of training multiple
          times a week rather than as an occasional challenge.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">4.25</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">22x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">-3/15%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Decline/Incline</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">24"</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">HD Touchscreen</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's the full spec sheet, taken from NordicTrack's published specifications for the Commercial 2450.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Expect assembly to be close to the 1750's but a bit longer thanks to the larger 24-inch touchscreen mast, which the manual recommends two people lift into place. The console arrives mostly pre-wired, and it's worth checking that the included tools cover every step before you begin.
        </p>
        <p>
          The shipping crate is heavier than the 1750's given the larger console, so plan your delivery location accordingly — this isn't a machine you want to carry up a flight of stairs in its original box. Once assembled, run a basic calibration check: full incline and decline range, a safety key check, and a firmware check on the touchscreen before the first real workout.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck: Where the Upgrade Actually Shows Up</h3>
        <p>
          This is the section that matters most for anyone deciding between
          the 2450 and the 1750. During moderate walking and easy jogging, both motors are working well within their ratings, so the difference between 3.75 and 4.25 CHP is unlikely to matter. Where extra continuous horsepower earns its place is under sustained load — fast running combined with repeated steep climbs — because a stronger motor has more reserve to hold belt speed as the load changes. It's a modest difference on paper, but it's the kind of headroom a runner training seriously makes use of over weeks of repeated hard sessions, even if a casual user never would.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 22 x 60-inch deck is identical to the 1750's, so the upgrade
          here is entirely about motor strength and incline ceiling, not
          running surface. A 60-inch length is generous enough for most runners at full stride on either machine.
        </p>
        <h3>Incline, Decline, and Hill Training</h3>
        <p>
          The 2450's 15% max incline versus the 1750's 12% is the single
          biggest functional difference between the two machines, and it's
          the reason we'd recommend the 2450 to anyone doing dedicated hill
          training. For a structured hill-repeat session — alternating between flat recovery jogging and 12-15% incline climbs — the steeper ceiling allows genuinely demanding terrain that simply isn't available on the 1750. Decline is shared at -3%
          between both models, useful for downhill-specific training or
          simply varying the stimulus during long iFit trail runs.
        </p>
        <h3>The 24-Inch Touchscreen</h3>
        <p>
          The step up from the 1750's 14-inch screen to a 24-inch display is
          noticeable immediately, closer in spirit to a small television
          than a large tablet. iFit's trainer-led trail runs are designed around video, and a larger screen makes that video a bigger part of the session, which may make a scenic guided run more appealing than a manual one. It's a smaller jump
          than the 2950's 32-inch screen, but it's the point on the
          NordicTrack lineup where screen size starts to feel like a genuine
          amenity rather than a marketing footnote.
        </p>
        <h3>Cushioning, Console, and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          FlexSelect cushioning works identically to the 1750, with the same
          three firmness settings via a lever under the deck. As a general rule, the softest setting suits joint-friendly daily walking, while the firmest setting gives a more responsive surface for faster interval sessions. The built-in speakers and Bluetooth audio are the same as on the 1750 — fine for a small room, nothing exceptional either.
        </p>
        <h3>Folding and Footprint</h3>
        <p>
          The EasyLift assist strut helps the same way it does across the
          Commercial line, though the larger touchscreen mast makes the 2450 marginally heavier to fold than the 1750. Standing
          folded, its 37 x 83-inch footprint is only slightly larger than
          the 1750's, so if folding for storage matters to you, the
          difference between these two specific models won't be the
          deciding factor.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise, Vibration, and Household Fit</h3>
        <p>
          We haven't measured the 2450's noise ourselves. A stronger motor isn't automatically a louder one — at a given speed, a motor with more reserve is working further from its limit — and since the deck and frame are shared with the 1750, there's no reason to expect a meaningful difference in noise or floor vibration between the two. If noise or vibration is a concern, upgrading to the 2450 isn't likely to change that calculus one way or the other — a mat underneath still helps on
          hard flooring regardless of which model you choose.
        </p>
        <h3>Real-World Training Scenarios</h3>
        <p>
          Think about three training scenarios that map closely to how serious home runners actually use a treadmill: a long tempo run at a fixed fast pace, a hill-repeat session cycling between flat recovery and 12-15% climbs, and a long run mixing iFit-guided terrain with manual pace changes. The hill-repeat session is the most demanding of the three for any motor, because sudden incline changes put the most torque-varying load on it, and only the 2450 reaches the top of that incline range at all. This is the kind of workout mix where the extra CHP over the 1750 earns its keep most clearly.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          We have not tested the 2450 ourselves, and a spec sheet can't answer a multi-year durability question, so we looked at patterns in verified owner reviews spanning one to two
          years of ownership. The stronger motor shows up favorably here:
          owners who use the 2450 for serious, frequent running reported
          fewer motor-strain complaints than we've seen on the 1750 under
          comparably heavy use, consistent with the idea that the extra CHP
          provides genuine reserve capacity rather than just a bigger number
          on a spec sheet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Belt tracking and tension remain the most common maintenance item
          reported by owners, in line with essentially every treadmill we've
          reviewed regardless of price. The touchscreen and console
          electronics showed a low complaint rate, and the frame drew
          consistent praise for staying tight and rattle-free even under
          frequent hard training. NordicTrack's warranty on this model
          matches the 1750's — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts, and 1-year
          labor policy — despite the higher price point, which is worth
          knowing if warranty coverage is a factor in your decision between
          the two.
        </p>
        <p>
          As with every NordicTrack model, keeping the belt lubricated on
          the manufacturer's suggested schedule remains the single most
          effective thing an owner can do to avoid premature wear, and it
          matters just as much on the 2450 as it does on any cheaper model
          in the lineup.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Pros and Cons</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <div class="bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-4">
