        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          It's also worth flagging a subtler group who should skip the
          2450: shoppers who are buying based on a spec sheet comparison
          alone rather than their actual training history. It's easy to
          look at "4.25 CHP versus 3.75 CHP" and assume more is always
          better, but the honest truth is that the extra reserve is unlikely to matter unless you're regularly pushing pace or incline hard enough to work the 1750 near its limit.
          If you've never owned a treadmill before and don't yet know your
          own training habits, we'd suggest starting with the 1750 and
          upgrading later if you find yourself wanting more — it's a far
          cheaper mistake to correct than the reverse.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your training is mostly flat-ground walking or easy jogging, you are unlikely to notice the difference between this machine and the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/">Commercial 1750</a>,
          and the extra $500 is better spent elsewhere or simply saved. If
          you're a serious runner already leaning toward the strongest
          possible motor and largest possible screen regardless of price,
          skip straight to the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2950-treadmill/">Commercial 2950</a>
          rather than treating the 2450 as a stepping stone you'll want to
          upgrade from again later. And if you're still comparing broadly
          across budgets, our <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews/">best treadmills for home</a>
          guide is a better starting point than committing to a specific
          NordicTrack tier this early in your research.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more consideration worth weighing: financing and resale.
          Because the 2450 sits at a genuinely popular mid-flagship price
          point, it tends to hold resale value slightly better than either
          the entry-level 1750 or the far pricier 2950, both of which see
          steeper depreciation curves in the used market — the 1750 because
          it's the highest-volume model with the most secondhand supply, and
          the 2950 because its price ceiling narrows the pool of buyers
          willing to pay used-market prices for a flagship. If resale value
          factors into your decision at all, that's a small point in the
          2450's favor.
        </p>
