        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack Commercial 2450 does exactly what it sets out to
          do: it takes everything that already works about the Commercial
          1750 and adds real, usable headroom for runners who need it. The
          stronger motor and steeper incline aren't marketing dressing —
          for hard sessions they are the two specs that matter most, and runners doing serious hill training will make real use of both.
        </p>
        <p>
          The honest caveat is the same one we'd give about any mid-tier
          upgrade: buy this because your training demands it, not because
          it's the next model up from the one you were already considering.
          For walkers and easy joggers, the 1750 remains the smarter buy.
          For runners who regularly push pace and incline, the 2450 is worth
          every one of the extra dollars.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're still on the fence after reading this far, our practical
          advice is to think back over your last few months of actual
          training rather than your aspirational training plan. Runners
          consistently underestimate how much of their weekly mileage is
          genuinely easy-paced, flat-ground running, which the 1750 handles
          just as well as the 2450 does. Buy the upgrade for the training
          you're actually doing today, not the training you might do
          someday — you can always trade up later once your mileage and
          incline demands catch up to the machine.
        </p>
