        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 worth the upgrade from the 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              For serious runners who do regular hill training or push faster paces often, yes. The 4.25 CHP motor and 15% max incline versus the 1750's 3.75 CHP and 12% give you real headroom for hard sessions. For walkers and moderate joggers, the 1750 already covers the same ground and the 2450's extra $500 is harder to justify.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the actual difference between the Commercial 2450 and the Commercial 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The 2450 steps up to a 4.25 CHP motor from 3.75 CHP, extends
              max incline from 12% to 15%, and swaps the 1750's 14-inch
              touchscreen for a 24-inch display. Deck size and weight
              capacity are identical between the two, so the upgrade is
              concentrated in motor strength, incline range, and screen
              size.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Commercial 2450 good for incline or hill training?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes, this is where the 2450 earns its price most clearly. The 15% max incline is steep enough for genuinely demanding simulated climbs, and the stronger motor gives more reserve for sustained incline work, which is exactly the scenario where a smaller motor like the 1750's is working closer to the top of its range.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the Commercial 2450 compare to the Commercial 2950?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The 2950 adds a stronger 4.5 CHP motor and a much larger 32-inch touchscreen for roughly $1,700-$2,200 more, while incline range, deck size, and cushioning stay the same. On paper the gap between a 4.25 and a 4.5 CHP motor is small, and most runners, including serious ones, are unlikely to need the extra reserve, which makes the 2450 the better value for all but the most dedicated buyers.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is the Commercial 2450 at higher speeds?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              We haven't measured its noise ourselves. A stronger motor doing the same work is operating further from its limit, so there's no reason to expect it to be meaningfully louder than the Commercial 1750 at the same speed. At running pace, footfall on the deck is usually the bigger source of sound on any treadmill, and a mat helps on hard floors.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take on the Commercial 2450?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Plan for it to take a little longer than the Commercial 1750 because of the larger 24-inch touchscreen mast, which the manual recommends two people lift into place. The console and mast arrive mostly pre-wired, which keeps the process manageable even for a first-time treadmill builder.
            </p>
